by GromNaN at 2026-08-29T09:12:15Z

Rebased on 8.2 and reworked the design, to remove the `protected` extension points and split schema resolution from validation.

The component now has a `Symfony\Component\Yaml\Schema` namespace:

* `SchemaResolverInterface::resolve($content, $file)` returns the schema a document must validate against, or null.
* `FileHeaderSchemaResolver` reads the `# yaml-language-server: $schema=` and `# $schema=` header, resolving a relative location against the directory of the file.
* `SchemaValidatorInterface` exposes `isSupported()` and `validate($data, $schema, $content)`.
* `SchemaValidator` implements it on top of `opis/json-schema`, and is the only class that depends on the library. It no longer resolves anything, so it has no constructor and no state to configure.

`LintCommand` receives a resolver and a validator through its constructor, plus the base directory schema paths are displayed relative to. All three arguments are optional and appended at the end of the signature. `protected $schema`, `createSchemaValidator()` and `getSchemaBaseDir()` are gone.

FrameworkBundle ships `YamlLintSchemaResolver`, which decorates the header resolver and falls back to Symfony conventions. It no longer reaches for the kernel through `getApplication()`, and it is wired as an inline service in `console.php` so nothing is left dangling when `FrameworkExtension` removes the command.

Two changes to the default resolution:

* The `.yml` extension is accepted, not just `.yaml`.
* The generated `schema.json` is only applied to files inside the `packages` directory of the project config directory. It describes the configuration of this application, so it must not be applied to a `config/packages` belonging to a bundle or to another project. The component schemas of well known files stay unanchored, since the naming convention also holds inside bundles.

The config directory comes from the `.kernel.config_dir` build parameter, the same one `JsonSchemaConfigDumpPass` uses to write the file. It stays optional: when the kernel does not expose one, the generated schema is simply never applied.

New unit tests cover the header resolver, the FrameworkBundle resolver (well known files, files outside the conventions, Windows paths, the decorated resolver taking precedence, `config/packages` with and without the generated file) and the container wiring with and without `.kernel.config_dir`.

